# Line-ending policy for files that Linux containers and /bin/sh consume.
#
# Why this file exists: a Windows checkout made with the installer default
# `core.autocrlf=true` converts every LF file to CRLF in the working tree. For
# shell scripts that means `#!/bin/sh\r`, which a Linux container refuses to
# execute — the production ClamAV image (built from `deploy/clamd/`) and the
# API image (built from `apps/api/`) would both fail at start. This was
# observed on a real Windows 11 host on 2026-10-30; see
# docs/LOCAL_WINDOWS_VERIFICATION_REPORT.md, defect D1.
#
# The rules below override `core.autocrlf` per path (`eol=lf` forces LF both on
# check-in and on check-out, on every platform), so a fresh Windows clone gets
# LF in these files regardless of the local git configuration. Everything NOT
# listed here keeps the user's normal settings — Windows-native files
# (PowerShell, .bat, Visual Studio files) are deliberately untouched.
#
# Regression guard: tools/security_baseline.py asserts these rules exist and
# that every tracked file they cover is LF in the working tree.

# Shell scripts — executed by /bin/sh inside Linux containers or on POSIX CI.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Container image definitions and their COPY-ed configs.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
deploy/clamd/*.conf text eol=lf
